# Organizing Widgets with Frames

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- **Frame widget**: container that holds other widgets
  - Useful for organizing and arranging groups of widgets in a window
  - The contained widgets are added to the frame widget which contains them
  - Example: `tkinter.Label(self.top_frame, text='hi')`
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{figures/ch13-fig-13-19-frame-arrangement}
\label{fig:ch13-19-frame-arrangement}
\caption{Arrangement of widgets using a frame \parencite[pg.669]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# `Button` Widgets

::: columns
:::: {.column width=33%}
::: {.block}
## Buttons

You use the `Button` widget to create a standard button in a window.
When the user clicks a button, a specified function or method is called
:::
:::{.block}
## Callback function
A callback function is also known as an **event handler** because it
handles the event that occurs when the user clicks the button.
:::
::::
:::: {.column width=66%}
- **`Button` widget**: widget that the user can click to cause an action
  to take place
  - When creating a button you can specify:
    - Text to appear on the face of the button
    - A **callback function**

\vspace{0.4in}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/ch13-fig-13-20-button}
\label{fig:ch13-20-button}
\caption{Example of a tkinter Button \parencite[pg.671]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# Info Dialog Boxes

::: columns
:::: {.column width=66%}
- **Info dialog box**: a dialog box that shows information to the user
  - Format for creating an info dialog box:
  - `import tkinter.messagebox` module
   - `tkinter.messagebox.showinfo(title, message)`
    - `title` is displayed in the dialog box's title bar
    - `message` is an informational string displayed in the main part
      of the dialog box
::::
:::: {.column width=33%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{figures/ch13-fig-13-21-info-dialog}
\label{fig:ch13-21-info-dialog}
\caption{Example of a tkinter messagebox info dialog box \parencite[pg.671]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# Creating a Quit Button

- **`Quit` button**: closes the program when the user clicks it
- To create a quit button in Python `tkinter`:
  - Create a `Button` widget
  - Set the root widget's `destroy()` method as the callback function
  - When the user clicks the button the `destroy()` method is called
    and the program ends

# Getting Input with the `Entry` Widget

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
::: {.block}
## `Entry` Widget
An `Entry` widget is a rectangular area that the user can type input
into.  You use the `Entry` widget's `get()` method to retrieve the data
that has been typed into the widget.
:::
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.4]{figures/ch13-fig-13-25-entry-widget}
\label{fig:ch13-25-entry-widget}
\caption{Example of entry widget and result dialog \parencite[pg.675]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
- **`Entry` widget**: rectangular area that the user can type text into
  - Used to gather input in a GUI program
  - Typically followed by a button for submitting the data
    - The button's callback function retrieves the data from the `Entry`
      widget and processes it
  - **`Entry` widget's `get()` method**: used to retrieve the data from
    an `Entry` widget
    - Returns a string
::::
:::

# Using Labels as Output Fields

::: columns
:::: {.column width=33%}
::: {.block}
## `StringVar` and `Label`
When a `StringVar` object is associated with a `Label` widget, the 
`Label` widget displays any data that is stored in the `StringVar`
object.
:::
\vspace{0.25in}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{figures/ch13-fig-13-27-stringvar-label}
\label{fig:ch13-27-stringvar-label}
\caption{Example of StringVar used to dynamically display result in a Label \parencite[pg.678]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=66%}
- Can use `Label` widgets to dynamically display output
  - Used to replace info dialog box
  - Create empty `Label` widget in main window, and write code that
    displays desired data in the label when a button is clocked

- **`StringVar` class**: `tkinter` module class that can be used along
  with a `Label` widget to display data
  - Create `StringVar` object then create `Label` widget and associate
    it with the `StringVar` object
  - Subsequently, any value stored in the `StringVar` object will
    automatically be displayed in the `Label` widget
::::
:::

# Summary

- A `Frame` is a container that holds other widgets.  You use `Frames`
  in most GUI frameworks to organize the widgets in a window (GUI layout)
- A `Button` widget is a user clickable item that can be used to generate events
- A **callback function** or an **event handler** are called asynchronously by the
  GUI event loop when events happen.
  - For example you can associate a callback function to be called when a button press even
    occurs.
- Dialog boxes, like the `tkinter` `messagebox` are useful for popping up information for
  users.
- The `Entry` widget provides a basic ability to enter user text in a `tkinter` GUI application
  - Can use the `get()` method of the `Entry` object to get the text
  - Can then convert to types like `int` or `float` using built-in Python methods
- In `tkinter` a `StringVar` can be used in combination with a `Label` to
  dynamically display output generated by the GUI application.


